# C# Test Automation Conventions

Follow [docs/csharp-test-style-guide.md](../../docs/csharp-test-style-guide.md) when adding or changing tests.

## Naming

- Test methods: `Action_Condition_ExpectedOutcome`
- Test classes: `{Feature}{Layer}Tests`
- Tag every test with layer (`Api`, `Ui`, `E2E`) and purpose (`Smoke`, `Regression`, `Contract`) as appropriate

## Layer rules

- Default to API tests unless the risk is browser-specific (rendering, routing, interaction)
- Use E2E only when cross-layer state must be proven
- Never put locators or HTTP calls in test bodies — extend framework first

## Framework-first workflow

1. Add or extend API client, page object, or builder in `src/SeniorQaAutomation.Framework/`
2. Then write a thin test in `tests/SeniorQaAutomation.Tests/`
3. Copy from `docs/templates/*.cs.template` when starting a new file

## Test data

- Use `{Entity}Builder.CreateValid()` with fluent `With*` overrides
- Never hard-code dates, IDs, or names in test bodies
- Override only fields relevant to the scenario

## Assertions and cleanup

- Group related checks with `Assert.Multiple`
- Happy path: `.EnsureSuccess()` then assert fields; negative path: assert `StatusCode` directly
- Write assertion messages as behaviour statements, not technical labels
- Track created resource IDs and delete in TearDown/finally; log cleanup failures without failing the test
- Capture browser artifacts only on failure (see `UiTestBase`)

## Anti-patterns

- No `Thread.Sleep` — use explicit waits via `WaitHelper`
- No secrets in logs or assertion messages
- No custom assertion libraries beyond NUnit constraints
